 ## Overview
-Topological Correctness Guarantees is a family of isosurface extraction methods designed to resolve the topological ambiguities inherent in standard Marching Cubes [Marching Cubes](774-marching-cubes.md). Standard Marching Cubes uses linear interpolation, which can produce incorrect connectivity (e.g., disconnected sheets instead of a tunnel) when the underlying trilinear interpolant has critical points within a voxel [Topologically Correct Marching Cubes](1368-topologically-correct-marching-cubes.md). This family ensures the output mesh is homeomorphic to the trilinear interpolant of the input scalar field or a valid 2-manifold for binary data, preventing non-manifold artifacts and self-intersections [Topologically Defined Isosurfaces](1428-topologically-defined-isosurfaces.md). Unlike standard implementations that rely on arbitrary lookup tables, these methods use geometric tests, vertex insertion, or global connectivity constraints to deterministically resolve ambiguous configurations [Asymptotic Decider](790-asymptotic-decider.md)[Topologically Defined Isosurface via Connectedness Couples](1430-topologically-defined-isosurface-via-connectedness-couples.md).
